<section xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="s_invertibility_theorem">
  <title>The invertibility theorem</title>
  <introduction>
<p>
  We are now in a position to prove our first big theorem: the invertibilty theorem. Logically speaking, the theorem states that a number of conditions are all equivalent to a matrix <m>A</m> being invertible. The advantage of having such a diverse collection of equivalent conditions is that we are provided with a powerful tool for determining when a matrix is invertible, and for computing the inverse matrix when it is.
</p>
  </introduction>
<subsection xml:id="ss_invertibility_th">
    <title>The invertibility theorem</title>

<theorem xml:id="th_invertibility">
  <title>Invertibility theorem</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>.
      The following statements are equivalent.
      <ol>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_def">
          <p>
            <m>A</m> is invertible.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="inv_statement_unique_sol">
          <p>
            The matrix equation <md>A\underset{n\times 1}{\boldx}=\underset{n\times 1}{\boldb}</md> has a <em>unique solution</em> for any <m>\boldb\in \R^n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_trivial_sol">
          <p>
            The matrix equation <md>A\underset{n\times 1}{\boldx}=\underset{n\times 1}{\boldzero}</md> has a unique solution: namely, <m>\boldx=\boldzero_{n\times 1}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_row_equiv">
          <p>
            <m>A</m> is row equivalent to <m>I_n</m>,
            the <m>n\times n</m> identity matrix.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_prod_elems">
          <p>
            <m>A</m> is a product of elementary matrices.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
      Recall that to show two statements <m>\mathcal{P}</m> and <m>\mathcal{Q}</m> are equivalent,
      we must show two implications:
      <m>\mathcal{P}\implies \mathcal{Q}</m>, and <m>\mathcal{Q}\implies \mathcal{P}</m>. Instead of doing this for each possible pair of sentences above,
      we ease our work load by instead showing the following
      <em>cycle</em> of implications:
      <md>
        (1)\implies(2)\implies(3)\implies(4)\implies(5)\implies (1)
      </md>.
      Since implication is transitive, starting at any point in our cycle and making our way around the chain of implications, we see that any one of the propositions implies any other proposition.
    </p>
    <case>
     <title><m>(1)\implies (2)</m></title>
    <p>
    Suppose <m>A^{-1}</m> exists. Given any column vector <m>\boldb</m>, we have
    <md>
    <mrow>
      A\boldx=\boldb \amp \iff \boldx=A^{-1}\boldb \amp (<xref ref="cor_solving_invertible"/>)
      </mrow>
    </md>,
    which shows that <m>\boldx=A^{-1}\boldb</m> is the unique solution to <m>A\boldx=\boldb</m>.
    </p>
  </case>
  <case>
   <title><m>(2)\implies (3)</m></title>
  <p>
  Clearly, if <m>A\boldx=\boldb</m> has a unique solution for <em>any</em> choice of <m>\boldb</m>, then it has a unique solution for the <em>particular</em> choice <m>\boldb=\boldzero</m>. Since <m>\boldx=\boldzero_{n\times 1}</m> is clearly a solution to the equation, it must be the only solution.
</p>
  </case>
  <case>
   <title><m>(3)\implies (4)</m></title>
  <p>
  Row reduce <m>A</m> to a matrix <m>U</m> in <em>reduced</em> row echelon form using Gauss-Jordan elimination. (See <xref ref="th_matrixforms"/>.) Since the set of solutions to <m>A\boldx=\boldzero</m> is identical to the set of solutions to <m>U\boldx=\boldzero</m> (apply <xref ref="s_systems_th_rowops"/> to their corresponding linear systems), we see that <m>\boldzero</m> is the only solution to <m>U\boldx=\boldzero</m>. <xref ref="proc_solveSystem"/> now implies <m>U</m> has a leading one in each column. Since <m>U</m> is <m>n\times n</m> and in <em>reduced</em> row echelon form, it follows that <m>U</m> must be the identity matrix. (Convince yourself of this.) Thus <m>A</m> is row equivalent to <m>U=I</m>, the identity matrix.
  </p>
  </case>
  <case>
 <title><m>(4)\implies (5)</m></title>
  <p>
  If <m>A</m> is row equivalent to <m>I</m>, then according to our discussion after <xref ref="eq_GE_via_elem_mat"/>, we have <m>I=E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1A</m> for some collection of elementary matrices <m>E_i</m>. Since elementary matrices are invertible we can multiply both sides of this equation by
  <md>(E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1)^{-1}=E_1^{-1}E_{2}^{-1}\cdots E_r^{-1}</md> to conclude
  <md>
    A=E_1^{-1}E_{2}^{-1}\cdots E_r^{-1}
  </md>.
  Since inverses of elementary matrices are elementary (<xref ref="th_inverse_elem"/>), we conclude that <m>A</m> is a product of elementary matrices.
  </p>
  </case>
  <case>
   <title><m>(5)\implies (1)</m></title>
  <p>
  If <m>A</m> is a product of elementary matrices, then it is a product of invertible matrices. Since products of invertible matrices are invertible, we conclude that <m>A</m> is invertible.
  </p>
  </case>
  </proof>
</theorem>
    <remark xml:id="rm_inv_solutions">
  <statement>
    <p>
      The <xref ref="th_invertibility" text='custom'> invertibility theorem </xref> has an immediate application to linear systems where the number of equations is <em>equal to</em> the number of unknowns. In this special situation, the system is equivalent to a matrix equation of the form
      <md>
        A\boldx=\boldb
      </md>,
      where <m>A</m> is a <em>square matrix</em>. According to the theorem, if we know <m>A</m> is invertible, then the matrix equation, and hence the linear system, has a unique solution: namely, <m>\boldx=A^{-1}\boldb</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      What if <m>A</m> is not invertible? Then the theorem only tells us that there is some column vector <m>\boldc</m>, not necessarily the given <m>\boldb</m>, such that the equation
      <md>
        A\boldx=\boldc
      </md>
      does not have a unique solution. In other words, the theorem alone doesn't allow us to conclude whether the given <m>A\boldx=\boldb</m> has a solution, and we must resort to our usual Gaussian elimination procedure to answer this question.
    </p>
  </statement>
</remark>
<p>
  The family of <em>triangular</em> matrices (upper, lower, and diagonal) defined below provides an easy testing ground for our new invertibility theorem.
</p>
<definition xml:id="d_diagonal_triangular">
  <idx><h>matrices</h><h>diagonal</h></idx>
  <idx><h>matrices</h><h>triangular</h></idx>
  <idx><h>matrices</h><h>upper triangular</h></idx>
  <idx><h>matrices</h><h>lower triangular</h></idx>
  <title>Diagonal and triangular matrices</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A=[a_{ij}]</m> be <m>n\times n</m>.
      <ol type="i">
          <li>
            <p>
            For each <m>1\leq i\leq n</m> the entry <m>a_{ii}</m> is called the <m>i</m>-th <term>diagonal entry</term> of <m>A</m>, and subarray of <m>A</m> consisting of  <m>a_{11}, a_{22}, \dots, a_{nn}</m> is called the <term>diagonal</term>. An <term>off-diagonal</term> entry of <m>A</m> is any entry not among the diagonal entries.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
            The matrix <m>A</m> is <term>diagonal</term> if all off-diagonal entries are zero: <ie />,
            if <m>a_{ij}=0</m> for all <m>1\leq i,j\leq n</m> with <m>i\ne j</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The matrix <m>A</m> is <term>upper triangular</term>
            if all entries below the diagonal are zero: <ie />, if <m>a_{ij}=0</m> for all <m>1
            leq j\lt i</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The matrix <m>A</m> is <term>lower triangular</term>
            if all entries above the diagonal are zero: <ie />, if <m>a_{ij}=0</m> for all <m>1\leq i\lt j </m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            The matrix <m>A</m> is <term>triangular</term>
            if <m>A</m> is upper triangular or lower triangular.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>
<example xml:id="eg_triang_matrices">
  <title>Triangular <m>3\times 3</m> matrices</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      The set <m>W_1</m> of all diagonal <m>3\times 3</m> matrices can be described as
      <md>
        W_1=\left\{ \begin{amatrix}[ccc]r\amp 0\amp 0\\ 0\amp s\amp 0 \\ 0\amp 0\amp t  \end{amatrix}\colon r,s,t\in \R\right\}
      </md>.
      The set <m>W_2</m> of all upper triangular <m>3\times 3</m> matrices can be described as
      <md>
        W_2=\left\{ \begin{amatrix}[ccc]r\amp s\amp t\\ 0\amp u\amp v \\ 0\amp 0\amp w  \end{amatrix}\colon r,s,t,u,v,w\in \R\right\}
      </md>.
      The set <m>W_3</m> of all lower triangular <m>3\times 3</m> matrices can be described as
      <md>
        W_3=\left\{ \begin{amatrix}[ccc]r\amp 0\amp 0\\ s\amp t\amp 0 \\ u\amp v\amp w  \end{amatrix}\colon r,s,t,u,v,w\in \R\right\}
      </md>.

    </p>
  </statement>
</example>
<theorem xml:id="th_invertible_triangular">
  <title>Invertibility of triangular matrices</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A=[a_{ij}]</m> be a triangular <m>n\times n</m> matrix.
      Then <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if
      <m>a_{ii}\ne 0</m> for all <m>1\leq i\leq n</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      In other words,
      <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if the diagonal entries of <m>A</m> are all nonzero.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
      In this proof we will make use of the fact that a square matrix <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if <m>A^T</m> is invertible. (<xref ref="th_inverse_trans"/>)
    </p>
    <case>
     <title>Case: <m>A</m> is upper triangular</title>
    <p>
    If <m>a_{ii}\ne 0</m> for all <m>i</m>, then it is easy to see that we can row reduce <m>A</m> first to a row echelon matrix with leading ones in every diagonal entry, and then further to the identity matrix. Thus <m>A</m> is row equivalent to <m>I</m> in this case, and we conclude from statement (4) of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/> that <m>A</m> is invertible.
    </p>
    <p>
      For the other implication, we show that if it is not the case that <m>a_{ii}\ne 0</m> for all <m>i</m>, then there is a nonzero solution <m>\boldx\ne \boldzero</m> to the matrix equation <m>A\boldx=\boldzero</m>. If this is the case, then since we have two distinct solutions to <m>A\boldx=\boldzero</m>, <m>A</m> is not invertible by Statement (3) of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/>.
    </p>
    <p> To this end, assume it is not the case that <m>a_{ii}\ne 0</m> for all <m>i</m>. Then we can find a smallest index <m>k</m> such that <m>a_{kk}=0</m> and <m>a_{ii}\ne 0</m> for any <m>i &lt;k</m>. It is easy to see that <m>A</m> is row equivalent to a matrix <m>A'=[a'_{ij}]</m>, satisfying <m>a_{jj}=1</m>  for <m>1\leq j\leq k-1</m> and <m>a_{ij}=0</m> for all <m>1\leq i &lt; j\leq k-1</m>: i.e., <m>A'</m> is <q>diagonal up until the <m>k</m>-th column</q>.
  </p>
    <p>
    We now provide a nonzero solution <m>\boldx=[x_i]_{n\times 1}</m>to <m>A'\boldx=\boldzero</m>: namely, set <m>x_k=1</m>, <m>x_{i}=0</m> for all <m>i>k</m>, and <m>x_{i}=-a'_{ik}</m> for all <m>i &lt; k</m>. (Verify this for yourself, using the description above of <m>a'_{ij}</m> for <m>j\leq k-1</m>.) Since <m>A'</m> is row equivalent to <m>A</m>, the linear systems corresponding to <m>\begin{amatrix}[cc] A\amp \boldzero\end{amatrix}</m> and <m>\begin{amatrix}[cc] A' \amp \boldzero\end{amatrix}</m> have the same solutions. Hence <m>\boldx</m> is also a nonzero solution to <m>A\boldx=\boldzero</m>. We conclude that <m>A</m> is not invertible by Statement (3) of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/>. This concludes the proof of this implication.
    </p>
    </case>
    <case>
     <title>Case: <m>A</m> is lower triangular</title>
    <p>
    Set <m>B=A^T</m>. Then <m>B</m> is upper triangular, and <m>(B)_{ii}=(A)_{ii}=a_{ii}</m> for all <m>i</m>. Then
    <md>
      <mrow>A \text{ invertible} \amp \iff B=A^T \text{ invertible} \amp \text{(see statement at top )}</mrow>
      <mrow> \amp \iff a_{ii}=0 \text{ for all } i \amp \text{(by previous case)} </mrow>
    </md>.
    </p>
    </case>
  </proof>

</theorem>
</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="ss_invertibility_alg">
  <title>Invertibility algorithms</title>
  <p>
    The proof of the implication <m>(4)\implies (5)</m> of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/> can be expanded into an algorithm that (1) decides whether a given matrix <m>A</m> is invertible, and (2) computes <m>A^{-1}</m> if <m>A</m> is invertible.
  </p>
  <algorithm xml:id="proc_inv_alg">
    <title>Inverse algorithm</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m> To test for invertibility of <m>A</m> proceed as follows.
      
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
          Build the <m>n\times 2n</m> augmented matrix <m>\begin{amatrix}[c|c]A\amp I_n\end{amatrix}</m> and use Gaussian elimination to row reduce to the form <m> \begin{amatrix}[c|c]U\amp B\end{amatrix} </m>, where <m>U</m> is in row echelon form.
          </p>
          <p>
            The matrix <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if <m>U</m> has <m>n</m> leading ones.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>U</m> has <m>n</m> leading ones, row reduce further to a matrix of the form <m>\begin{amatrix}[c|c] I_n\amp C\end{amatrix}</m>. Then <m>A^{-1}=C</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        From the proof of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/> we know <m>A</m> is invertible if and only if <m>U</m> has <m>n</m> leading ones. The question remains as to why reducing the augmented matrix <m>\begin{amatrix}[c|c]A\amp I\end{amatrix}</m>  to <m>\begin{amatrix}[c|c]I\amp C\end{amatrix}</m> tells us that <m>A^{-1}=C</m>. Let <m>E_1, E_2, \dots, E_r</m> be the elementary matrices representing the row operations involved in this process. Then we have
        <md>
            E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1A=I_n
        </md>.
        After a little algebra, we see that
        <md>
          A^{-1}=E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1
        </md>.
        Since <m>C</m> is the result of applying same row operations to <m>I_n</m> we have
        <md>
          C=E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1I=E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1=A^{-1}
        </md>,
        as claimed.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </algorithm>
  <p>
    From the proof of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/> we also derive an algorithm for writing an invertible matrix as a product of elementary matrices. You should think of this as a first example of the great versatility of Gaussian elimination, as conveyed by our <xref ref="princ_GE"/>.
  </p>
  <algorithm xml:id="th_elem_matrices_alg">
    <title>Product of elementary matrices algorithm</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
      Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m> To (potentially) write <m>A</m> as a product of elementary matrices, proceed as follows.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Attempt to row reduce <m>A</m> to the identity, keeping track of your sequence of row operations in the form of elementary matrices.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If you are able to row reduce <m>A</m> to <m>I_n</m> with a sequence of row operatons corresponding to the elementary matrices <m>E_1, E_2, \dots, E_r</m>, then
            <md>
              A=E_1^{-1}E_2^{-1}\cdots E_{r}^{-1}.
            </md>
            Since the inverse of an elementary matrix is elementary, we have written <m>A</m> as a product of elementary matrices.

          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        See the proof of the implication <m>(4)\implies (5)</m> in <xref ref="th_invertibility"/>.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </algorithm>

<example xml:id="eg_inv_alg_example">
  <title>Inverse algorithms</title>
      <p>
      Let <md>A=\begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 2\amp 0\\ 1\amp 2\amp 1\\ -1\amp -1\amp 3 \end{amatrix} </md>.
      Combining both algorithms we can decide whether <m>A</m> is invertible, and if so, compute <m>A^{-1}</m> and write <m>A</m> as a product of elementary matrices.
    <md>
      <mrow>\begin{amatrix}[rrr|rrr]1\amp 2\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 0\\ 1\amp 2\amp 1\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\\ -1\amp -1\amp 3\amp 0\amp 0\amp 1 \end{amatrix}  \amp \xrightarrow{r_2-r_1} \begin{amatrix}[rrr|rrr]1\amp 2\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 0\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -1\amp 1\amp 0\\ -1\amp -1\amp 3\amp 0\amp 0\amp 1 \end{amatrix}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp \xrightarrow{r_3+r_1} \begin{amatrix}[rrr|rrr]1\amp 2\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 0\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -1\amp 1\amp 0\\ 0\amp 1\amp 3\amp 1\amp 0\amp 1 \end{amatrix}</mrow>

      <mrow>\text{ (three leading ones, thus invertible) }\amp \xrightarrow{r_2\leftrightarrow r_3} \begin{amatrix}[rrr|rrr]\boxed{1}\amp 2\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 0\\ 0\amp \boxed{1}\amp 3\amp 1\amp 0\amp 1\\ 0\amp 0\amp \boxed{1}\amp -1\amp 1\amp 0 \end{amatrix}</mrow>

      <mrow>\amp \xrightarrow{r_2-3r_3} \begin{amatrix}[rrr|rrr]1\amp 2\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 0\\ 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 4\amp -3\amp 1\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -1\amp 1\amp 0 \end{amatrix}</mrow>

      <mrow>\amp \xrightarrow{r_1-2r_2} \begin{amatrix}[rrr|rrr]1\amp 0\amp 0\amp -7\amp 6\amp -2\\ 0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 4\amp -3\amp 1\\ 0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -1\amp 1\amp 0 \end{amatrix}</mrow>
      </md>
      According to <xref ref="proc_inv_alg"/>, the computation shows that <m>A</m> is invertible and
      <md>
        A^{-1}=\begin{amatrix}[rrr]-7\amp 6\amp -2\\ 4\amp -3\amp 1\\ -1\amp 1\amp 0 \end{amatrix}
      </md>.
      Next, representing our row operations as elementary matrices, we see that
      <md>
        E_5E_4E_3E_2E_1A=I
      </md>,
      where
        <md>
          <mrow> E_1\amp =\begin{amatrix}[rrr] 1\amp 0\amp 0\\
            -1\amp 1\amp 0\\
            0\amp 0\amp 1\end{amatrix}  \amp   E_2\amp =\begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 0\amp 0\\
            0\amp 1\amp 0\\
            1\amp 0\amp 1\end{amatrix}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>E_3\amp =\begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 0\amp 0\\
            0\amp 0\amp 1\\
            0\amp 1\amp 0 \end{amatrix} \amp   E_4\amp =\begin{amatrix}[rrr] 1\amp 0\amp 0\\
            0\amp 1\amp -3\\
            0\amp 0\amp 1\end{amatrix}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>E_5\amp =\begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp -2\amp 0\\
            0\amp 1\amp 0\\
            0\amp 0\amp 1\end{amatrix}</mrow>
        </md>.
        We conclude that
        <md>
          <mrow>A \amp =E_1^{-1}E_2^{-1}E_3^{-1}E_4^{-1}E_5^{-1}</mrow>
          <mrow> \amp= \begin{amatrix}[rrr] 1\amp 0\amp 0\\
            1\amp 1\amp 0\\
            0\amp 0\amp 1\end{amatrix}
            \begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 0\amp 0\\
            0\amp 1\amp 0\\
            -1\amp 0\amp 1\end{amatrix}
            \begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 0\amp 0\\
              0\amp 0\amp 1\\
              0\amp 1\amp 0 \end{amatrix}
              \begin{amatrix}[rrr] 1\amp 0\amp 0\\
              0\amp 1\amp 3\\
              0\amp 0\amp 1\end{amatrix}
              \begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 2\amp 0\\
                0\amp 1\amp 0\\
                0\amp 0\amp 1\end{amatrix}
            </mrow>
        </md>.
    </p>
    </example>
    <example xml:id="eg_inv_alg_singular">
    <title>Inverse algorithm</title>
    <statement>
    <p>
    Use <xref ref="proc_inv_alg"/> to decide whether the matrix 
<md>A=\begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 2\amp 3\\ 4\amp 5\amp 6\\ 7\amp 8\amp 9 \end{amatrix}</md> is invertible.
    </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
    <p>
    Note that since in any case we do not half to produce an actual inverse, we can dispense with the super-sized augmented matrix described in <xref ref="proc_inv_alg"/>, and instead just row reduce <m>A</m> to row echelon form. We have 
    <md>
      <mrow>
        \begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 2\amp 3\\ 4\amp 5\amp 6\\ 7\amp 8\amp 9 \end{amatrix} \amp \xrightarrow{r_2-4r_1} \begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 2\amp 3\\ 0\amp -3\amp -6\\ 7\amp 8\amp 9 \end{amatrix} 
      </mrow>
      <mrow>\amp \xrightarrow{r_3-7r_1} \begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 2\amp 3\\ 0\amp -3\amp -6\\ 0\amp -6\amp -12 \end{amatrix} \amp </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \amp \xrightarrow{-\tfrac{1}{3}r_2} \begin{amatrix}[rrr]1\amp 2\amp 3\\ 0\amp 1\amp 2\\ 0\amp -6\amp -12 \end{amatrix}
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \amp \xrightarrow{r_3+6r_2} \begin{amatrix}[rrr]\boxed{1}\amp 2\amp 3\\ 0\amp \boxed{1}\amp 2\\ 0\amp 0\amp 0 \end{amatrix}
      </mrow>
    </md>.
    Since the row echelon form of <m>A</m> has no leading one in the third column, we conclude that <m>A</m> is not invertible.
    </p>
    </solution>
    </example>
    
    <example xml:id="vid_eg_inver_alg">
      <title>Video example: inverse algorithm</title>
      <figure xml:id="fig_vid_inver_alg">
      <caption>Video: inverse algorithm</caption>
      <video xml:id="vid_inver_alg" youtube="yB80PXgNl7E" />
      </figure>
    </example>
  </subsection>

<subsection xml:id="ss_invertible_loose_ends">
  <title>Some theoretical loose ends</title>
<p>
The two inveribility algorithms above are nice examples of how a <em>theoretical</em> result like our <xref ref="th_invertibility" text="custom">invertibility theorem </xref> can pay some serious <em>computational</em> dividends. Namely, thanks to the theory we have discovered a method of computing the inverse of a matrix that essentially boils down to row reduction.
</p>
<p>
   We finish this section with a number of theoretical implications that tie up some loose ends. The results below are all consequences in some way of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/>. The fist shows that in fact one one of the defining equalities <m>AB=I</m> or <m>BA=I</m> suffices to define the inverse of a matrix.
</p>
<corollary xml:id="cor_left-right_inverse">
  <title>Left-inverse if and only if right-inverse</title>

  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be <m>n\times n</m>. Then
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>BA=I_n\implies AB=I_n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>AB=I_n\implies BA=I_n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      In plain English: a matrix <m>B</m> is a <em>left-inverse</em> of <m>A</m> if and only if it is a <em>right-inverse</em> of <m>A</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
      It is enough to prove the first implication:
      the second then follows by exchanging the roles of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose <m>BA=I_n</m>.
      We first show that <m>A</m> is invertible.
      We have
      <md>
        <mrow> A\boldx=\boldzero\amp\implies BA\boldx=\boldzero </mrow>
        <mrow> \amp\implies I\boldx=\boldzero </mrow>
        <mrow>  \amp \implies \boldx=\boldzero </mrow>
      </md>.
      By Statement (3) of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/>, we conclude that <m>A</m> is invertible.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now that we know <m>A^{-1}</m> exists we have
      <md>
        <mrow>BA=I_n\amp \implies  BAA^{-1}=IA^{-1} </mrow>
        <mrow>  \amp \implies B=A^{-1}</mrow>
        <mrow>  \amp \implies AB=AA^{-1}=I_n </mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
  </proof>
</corollary>
<p>
  As a further consequence of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/>, we can at last strengthen the implication
  <md>
    A \text{ and }  B \text{ invertible }\implies AB \text{ invertibe}
  </md>
  to an equivalence.
</p>
  <corollary xml:id="cor_inv_prod_eq">
    <title>Invertibility of product equivalence</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be <m>n\times n</m>.
        Then <m>AB</m> is invertible if and only if <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are both invertible: <ie />,
        <md>
          A \text{ and }  B \text{ invertible }\iff AB \text{ invertible}
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <case>
       <title>Implication: <m>A \text{ and } B \text{ invertible }\implies AB \text{ invertibe}</m></title>
       <p>
         We know from <xref ref="th_invertible_prod"/>  that if <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are invertible, then so is <m>AB</m>.
       </p>
      </case>
      <case>
       <title>Implication: <m>AB</m> invertible <m>\implies</m> <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> invertible</title>
       <p>
          Assume <m>AB</m> is invertible and let <m>C</m> be its inverse. Thus <m>C(AB)=(AB)C=I_n</m>.
         We first prove <m>B</m> is invertible. We have
         <md>
           <mrow>B\boldx=\boldzero\amp \implies  AB\boldx=\boldzero </mrow>
           <mrow>\amp \implies  \boldx=\boldzero</mrow>
         </md>.
        The last implication uses Statement (3) of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/> and the fact that <m>AB</m> is invertible. We have shown that
        <md>
          B\boldx=\boldzero\implies \boldx=\boldzero
        </md>,
        and hence that <m>B</m> is invertible, using once again Statement (3) of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/>.
       </p>
       <p>
         Next we prove directly that <m>A</m> is invertible. Namely, we claim that <m>A^{-1}=BC</m>.
         Indeed, since <m>C</m> is the inverse of <m>AB</m>, we have <m>A(BC)=(AB)C=I</m>. Thus <m>BC</m> is a right-inverse of <m>A</m>. <xref ref="cor_left-right_inverse"/> now implies <m>(BC)A=I</m>, and hence that <m>A^{-1}=BC</m>, as claimed.
       </p>
       <p>
         This completes the proof that if <m>AB</m> is invertible, then <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are invertible.
       </p>
      </case>

    </proof>
  </corollary>
<p>
  We conclude with two results related to row reduction. The first provides an equivalent formulation of row equivalence in terms of matrix arithmetic: an elaboration of our discussion in <xref ref="ss_systems_to_matrix_eqns"/>.
</p>
<corollary xml:id="cor_row_equivalence_invertibility">
  <title>Row equivalence and invertible matrices</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be <m>m\times n</m> matrices. The following statements are equivalent.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            The matrices <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are row equivalent.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            There are elementary matrices <m>E_1, E_2, \dots, E_r</m> such that 
            <m>
              E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1A=B
            </m>. 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            There is an invertible <m>m\times m</m> matrix <m>Q</m> such that <m>QA=B</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
   <p>
    The equivalence of (1) and (2) was shown in the course of our discussion in <xref ref="ss_systems_to_matrix_eqns"/>. The equivalence of (2) and (3) is a direct consequence of (5) of <xref ref="th_invertibility"/> since a matrix <m>Q</m> is invertible if and only if it can be written as
    <md>
      Q=E_rE_{r-1}\cdots E_1
    </md>
    for some elementary matrices <m>E_i</m>.
   </p> 
  </proof>
</corollary>
<p>As another corollary, we enlarge our invertibility theorem by three new statements. 
</p>
<corollary xml:id="cor_invertibility_enlarged">
  <title>Invertibility theorem: first expansion</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>A\in \ML_{n,n}(F)</m>.
      The following statements are equivalent.
      <ol>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_def">
          <p>
            <m>A</m> is invertible.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li xml:id="inv_statement_unique_sol">
          <p>
            The matrix equation <md>A\underset{n\times 1}{\boldx}=\underset{n\times 1}{\boldb}</md> has a <em>unique solution</em> for any <m>\boldb\in \R^n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_exists_sol">
          <p>
            The matrix equation <md>A\underset{n\times 1}{\boldx}=\underset{n\times 1}{\boldb}</md> has a solution for any <m>\boldb\in \R^n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
    <li xml:id="inv_statement_span">
          <p>
            The columns of <m>A</m> are a spanning tuple for <m>F^n</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_trivial_sol">
          <p>
            The matrix equation <md>A\underset{n\times 1}{\boldx}=\underset{n\times 1}{\boldzero}</md> has a unique solution: namely, <m>\boldx=\boldzero_{n\times 1}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_lin_indep">
          <p>
            The columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_row_equiv">
          <p>
            <m>A</m> is row equivalent to <m>I_n</m>,
            the <m>n\times n</m> identity matrix.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li xml:id="inv_statement_prod_elems">
          <p>
            <m>A</m> is a product of elementary matrices.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </statement>
</corollary>
<p>
  Lastly, we provide at last the proof of the third statement of <xref ref="th_matrixforms"/> promised back in <xref ref="s_systems_solve"/>. This proof is due to Thomas Yuster<fn>Thomas Yuster. <em>The reduced row echelon form of a matrix is unique: a simple proof</em>, Mathematics Magazine <term>57</term> (1984), no. 2, 93-94. </fn>.
</p>
<corollary xml:id="cor_RRE_uniqueness">
  <title>Uniqueness of reduced row echelon form</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Any matrix <m>A</m> is row equivalent to a <em>unique</em> matrix in reduced row echelon form.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <proof>
    <p>
      Let <m>A</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix. Using Gauss-Jordan elimination, we can row reduce <m>A</m> to a matrix <m>U</m> in reduced row echelon form. Suppose <m>A</m> is also row equivalent to the matrix <m>V</m> in reduced row echelon form; then <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> are row equivalent, since the row equivalence relation is symmetric and transitive. Thus it suffices to show that if <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> are row equivalent matrices in reduced row echelon form, then <m>U=V</m>. We do so by induction on <m>n</m>. The base step is trivial, since there are only two <m>m\times 1</m> matrices in reduced row echelon form: namely <m>\boldzero_{m\times 1}</m> and the column vector with a one in the first row and zeros elsewhere. 
    </p>
    <p>
      For the induction step we assume that any two row equivalent <m>m\times n</m> matrices in reduced row echelon form are equal. Let <m>U=[u_{ij}]</m> and <m>V=[v_{ij}]</m> be row equivalent <m>m\times (n+1)</m> matrices in reduced row echelon form, and let <m>\boldu_j</m> and <m>\boldv_j</m> denote the columns of <m>U</m> and <m>V</m>, respectively. By <xref ref="cor_row_equivalence_invertibility"/> there is an invertible matrix <m>Q</m> such that <m>QU=U'</m>. The first <m>n</m> columns of <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> form matrices <m>U'</m> and <m>V'</m>, respectively, that are in reduced row echelon form, as one easily checks. Furthermore, <m>U'</m> and <m>V'</m> are row equivalent: indeed, since <m>Q\boldu_j=\boldv_j</m> for all <m>1\leq j\leq n+1</m>, we see that <m>QU'=V'</m>, and thus <m>U'</m> and <m>V'</m> are row equivalent. By the induction hypothesis we must have <m>U'=V'</m>, and it remains only to show that <m>\boldu_{n+1}=\boldv_{n+1}</m>. We treat two cases: the case where one of <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> has a no leading one in its last column, and the case where both matrices have a leading one in their last column. 
    </p>
    <p>
      In the first case, suppose without loss of generality that the last column of <m>U</m> does not have a leading one. Consider the linear system with augmented matrix <m>[U\mid \boldx]</m>. Since the last column of <m>U</m> is free, it follows from <xref ref="proc_solveSystem"/> that for all <m>t\in \R</m> there is a solution to this system of the form <m>\boldx=(f_1(t),f_2(t),\dots, f_n(t),t)</m>, where <m>f_j(t)</m> is some function of <m>t</m>. Since  <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> are row equivalent, so are <m>[U\mid \boldzero]</m> and <m>[V\mid \boldzero]</m>, and thus the two systems corresponding to these augmented matrices have the same solution sets. It follows that for all <m>t\in \R</m>, we have <m>U\boldx_t=V\boldx_t=\boldzero</m>. Using the column method, we conclude that 
      <md>
        f_1(t)\boldu_1+\cdots +f_{n}(t)\boldu_n+t\bold_{n+1}=f_1(t)\boldv_1+\cdots +f_{n}(t)\boldv_n+t\bold_{n+1}
      </md>
      for all <m>t\in \R</m>. Since <m>U'=V'</m>, we have <m>\boldu_j=\boldv_j</m> for all <m>1\leq j\leq n</m>, and thus conclude that <m>t\boldu_{n+1}=t\boldv_{n_1}</m> for all <m>t\in \R</m>. Taking <m>t=1</m>, we conclude that <m>\boldu_{n+1}=\boldv_{n+1}</m>, and hence that <m>U=V</m> in this case.
    </p>
    <p>
      Finally, we consider the case where  <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> both have leading ones in their last columns.  Since <m>U</m> and <m>U'</m> are in reduced row echelon form, it follows that the leading one in the last column of <m>U</m> occurs in the row of <m>U</m> whose corresponding row in <m>U'</m> is the first zero row of <m>U'</m>. Since the same statement <foreign>mutatis mutandi</foreign> applies to <m>V</m> and <m>V'</m>, and since <m>U'=V'</m>, we see that the leading ones in the last columns of <m>U</m> and <m>V</m> occur in the same row. Since all other entries in that last column must be zero, we conclude that <m>\boldu_{n+1}=\boldv_{n+1}</m>, and hence that <m>U=V</m> in this case as well. 
    </p>
  </proof>

</corollary>

</subsection>

